# Fixed 2026-10-29, defect 2255, lane c-boundary (panel 232's R3): C hands the
# program a handle of another type at an address the program gave back, as a
# library recycling one block across kinds of object does.
# The shape beside the ffi-pragmatist ran with it (`s9b`): two such handles
# compared, live, the comparison panel 232's route (a) would have aborted;
# it ran before the repair and runs after it.
# Cause: the runtime forgot a given-back address only when an `acquires`
# result, a `borrows` hand-back or a callback's argument brought it back
# (panel 177's item 2). Every handle C hands the program clears its mark now,
# which spec § 13's *unless C has since handed its address out again* says.

extern "fixedbugs-2255-another-type.h"
    record A tag ta
    record B tag tb
    function a_open() -> A acquires a_close
    function a_close(x: A consumes)
    function b_get() -> B
    function b_read(x: B) -> i64

function main()
    a = a_open()
    a_close(x: a)
    b = b_get()
    c = b_get()
    print("b == c: ", b == c, ", b_read: ", b_read(x: c))
